                <pb facs="0698" n="608" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg608"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in 1 in No. 731.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Nunn to General Schenck.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate-General of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1875-02-12"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 12, 1875</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you in reference
                    to the consul-general’s letter of yesterday’s date respecting the ship “G. C.
                    Trufant,” of Newburyport, that the owner, or supposed owner, Mr. Gilbert Carr
                    Trufant, is a native-born citizen of the United States, and has been in business
                    in London for many years, the firm being Messrs. Randall &amp; Trufant, 144
                    Minories. I am not aware that Mr. Trufant has become a subject of Great
                    Britain.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. NUNN,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Vice and Deputy
                            Consul-General</hi>.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>His Excellency Gen. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert C.
                            Schenck</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States Minister,
                            London</hi>.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
